module geic.mojom;

import "chrome/browser/pwc/pwc_features.mojom";

// The GEIC capability surface, exposed to the qualifying main frame of a GEIC
// PrivilegedWebContents (see //chrome/browser/pwc). The browser-side binder
// (//chrome/browser/geic) hands this interface out only to a frame that passes
// the shared privileged-frame gate AND whose blessed component is GEIC, so a
// glic (or any other component's) frame never receives it.
//
// The interface is intentionally empty scaffolding. The GEIC team owns this
// file and adds the real capability surface in follow-up CLs, each
// implemented end to end and carrying IPC security review (see the *.mojom
// rule in this directory's OWNERS).
[RuntimeFeature=pwc.mojom.features.kPrivilegedWebContents]
interface GeicApi {};

// GEiC Mojo Interface and Glic Fork Rationale:
//
// This interface is a fork of the Glic Mojo API, tailored for Gemini Enterprise
// in Chrome (GEiC). We maintain a separate interface rather than reusing
// Glic's directly for two primary reasons:
//
// 1. Semantic and architectural divergence: GEiC operates under distinct
//    enterprise requirements (e.g., enterprise account constraints, a policy-
//    driven configuration channel, and default StoragePartition handling).
//    Additionally, Glic's API surface is API_OWNERS-gated, which would require
//    cross-team review queues for GEiC-specific iterations. Owning the Mojo
//    interface allows GEiC to evolve its contract independently, while sharing
//    underlying browser machinery over time.
//
// 2. Embedding architecture (PrivilegedWebContents vs <webview>):
//    Glic embeds its remote UI inside a <webview> hosted within a
//    chrome-untrusted:// WebUI container. In Glic, the remote page communicates
//    via window.postMessage with the WebUI host, which translates messages to
//    Mojo IPCs. In contrast, GEiC leverages PrivilegedWebContents (see
//    //chrome/browser/pwc), eliminating the intermediary WebUI host layer. The
//    remote GE web app runs directly in the privileged main frame and speaks
//    Mojo directly to the browser process. Consequently, this interface is
//    designed as a direct Mojo IPC surface rather than a postMessage bridge
//    API.
//
// TODO(crbug.com/547979281): Follow up on
// sharing Glic data types and the underlying extraction machinery (e.g., tab
// validity checks, inner text extraction, and screenshot capture in
// //chrome/browser/glic/host/context/) rather than re-implementing them. These
// live outside GEiC's OWNERS, so convergence is deferred to a follow-up.

// Browser-side interface for the GE client running in a GEIC
// PrivilegedWebContents. Methods and types are added alongside their
// implementation in a follow-up CL.
interface GeicBrowserHost {};

// Client-side interface implemented by the GE client, for browser-initiated
// notifications. Methods are added alongside their implementation.
interface GeicClient {};
